<!-- ================= English ================= -->
<div class="en" lang="en" dir="ltr">
<header>
<h1>Curiosity</h1>
<p>I told you not to scan it, you scanned the QR</p>
</header>

<section>
<h2>You scanned it!</h2>
<p>The code said "don't scan me", yet you scanned it. That is the best example of curiosity: you wanted to know what is here. Welcome to the report.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>What is curiosity?</h2>
<p>Curiosity is a natural desire to know, to learn and to discover the unknown. It is what drives people to ask questions, experiment and search for answers. In psychology it is considered one of the main drivers of learning.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>Curiosity through history</h2>
<p>Greek philosophers such as Aristotle believed that philosophy begins with wonder and the desire to know. In the Renaissance, scholars such as Leonardo da Vinci were famous for their endless curiosity. Since the seventeenth century, experimental science has grown from asking questions and testing ideas. In Islamic civilization, reading and reflection were encouraged, and Muslim scholars made great achievements in medicine, astronomy and mathematics driven by this curiosity.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>Curiosity in psychology</h2>
<p>Psychologists divide curiosity into types. The first is diverse curiosity, where a person looks for everything new and different. The second is deep curiosity, where a person wants to dive into one subject until understanding it well. There is also what scientists call the "information gap": when you feel you know part of something and not the rest, a small tension arises that pushes you to look for the answer. Curiosity can also appear as attraction to the forbidden, as happened to you on this page.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>Curiosity in science</h2>
<p>Brain research has shown that curiosity activates the reward areas of the brain and increases the release of dopamine. This makes learning more enjoyable and improves memory. Studies have found that when a person is curious about a topic, they remember both the topic itself and the related information better.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>Benefits and risks</h2>
<p>Curiosity leads to innovation, scientific discovery and personal growth. Curious people often learn faster and adapt to change more easily. But curiosity without caution can put a person in danger, such as opening an unknown link or scanning an unknown code on the internet. So the best approach is to combine curiosity with caution.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>How do we nurture curiosity?</h2>
<p>Ask many questions and do not settle for a single answer. Try new things and read in different fields. Do not fear mistakes, because they are a natural part of learning. In childhood, parents and teachers should not be annoyed by a child's questions, because those questions are the foundation of thinking.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>Curiosity is the force that carried humankind from caves to outer space. Today, by scanning this code, you became part of this story. Thank you for your curiosity!</p>
</section>

